.Dd May 1, 2026
.Dt LOADNGO-DESKTOP 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm loadngo-desktop
.Nd direct wsdisplay desktop shell for loadngo on NetBSD
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm netbsd_wsdesktop
.Op Fl -device Ar display
.Op Fl -mouse Ar device
.Op Fl -keyboard Ar device
.Op Fl -fps Ar rate
.Op Fl -cursor-hz Ar rate
.Op Fl -seconds Ar count
.Op Fl -no-input
.Op Fl -continuous
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the first direct framebuffer desktop shell for loadngo on
.Nx .
The current binary is named
.Nm netbsd_wsdesktop .
It opens a
.Xr wsdisplay 4
framebuffer, renders a small desktop scene, reads keyboard and mouse input
from wscons devices, and drives timers and readiness through the loadngo
kqueue proactor.
.Pp
The desktop is intentionally small and operational rather than decorative.
It provides a launcher, draggable application windows, a cursor overlay, a
status bar, and a first Terminal application.
.Pp
The Terminal application starts a persistent
.Pa /bin/sh
process.
Command lines are edited inside the desktop and submitted to the shell when
Enter is pressed.
Shell stdout and stderr are read back through
proactor readiness and displayed in the Terminal window.
.Pp
The Terminal is currently line-oriented.
It is useful for normal shell
commands and persistent shell state such as
.Ic cd ,
but it is not yet a full tty or pty emulator.
Curses applications, job
control, terminal modes, and interactive programs that require a real
controlling terminal are outside the current milestone.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -device Ar display
Open the wsdisplay framebuffer at
.Ar display .
The default is
.Pa /dev/ttyE0 .
.It Fl -mouse Ar device
Read mouse events from
.Ar device .
The default is
.Pa /dev/wsmouse .
.It Fl -keyboard Ar device
Read keyboard events from
.Ar device .
The default is
.Pa /dev/wskbd .
.It Fl -fps Ar rate
Set the maximum full-scene presentation rate.
The default is 2 frames per second.
This is a damage cap, not an animation target.
.It Fl -cursor-hz Ar rate
Set the maximum cursor damage presentation rate.
The default is 60 Hz.
Cursor movement restores the previous cursor rectangle from the back buffer
and redraws only the cursor region on the mapped front buffer.
.It Fl -seconds Ar count
Stop the desktop after
.Ar count
seconds.
This is intended for bounded smoke tests over SSH.
.It Fl -no-input
Disable keyboard and mouse devices.
This is useful for display-only smoke tests.
.It Fl -continuous
Continuously request scene repaints.
This is a diagnostic mode for repaint testing and should not be used as the
normal desktop mode.
.El
.Sh KEYBOARD
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Terminal active
Printable ASCII characters edit the command line.
On USB
.Xr wskbd 4
devices, key-down events are translated through the US keycode fallback when
ASCII events are not emitted.
Enter submits the command to the persistent shell.
Backspace removes the previous character.
.It Other app active
Tab or Enter cycles apps.
Q or Escape quits.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /dev/wsmouse
.It Pa /dev/ttyE0
Default wsdisplay framebuffer device.
.It Pa /dev/wsmouse
Default wscons mouse event source.
.It Pa /dev/wskbd
Default wscons keyboard event source.
.It Pa /bin/sh
Shell process used by the Terminal application.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std netbsd_wsdesktop
.Sh EXAMPLES
Build the desktop on the
.Nx
target:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cargo build --offline -p loadngo-host-desktop --bin netbsd_wsdesktop
.Ed
.Pp
Run the desktop using default devices:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&./target/debug/netbsd_wsdesktop
.Ed
.Pp
Run a bounded smoke test:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&./target/debug/netbsd_wsdesktop --seconds 10
.Ed
.Pp
Run without input devices:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
\&./target/debug/netbsd_wsdesktop --no-input --seconds 10
.Ed
.Sh NOTES
The desktop keeps a native-format RAM back buffer for full scene rendering.
Full scene presentation copies a completed frame to wsdisplay.
Cursor motion
uses a smaller damage path: the previous cursor rectangle is restored from the
back buffer, and the new cursor is drawn directly on the mapped framebuffer.
.Pp
The
.Fl -fps
and
.Fl -cursor-hz
options are independent.
The default desktop is event-driven; it does not
redraw merely because time has passed.
.Pp
The wsdisplay mapping can behave like slow device memory on the current target.
Avoid raw mouse-event-rate full redraws.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr sh 1 ,
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr wsdisplay 4 ,
.Xr wskbd 4 ,
.Xr wsmouse 4
